1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,200
In Breath of the Wild, grass catches fire,

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:03,760
and rain puts it out.

3
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:07,180
Were these combinations authored separately for every scene?

4
00:00:07,180 --> 00:00:10,680
Motion calculations and state rules such as ignition are distinct.

5
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:11,960
The state system distinguishes

6
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:14,160
elements from materials.

7
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:16,380
Elements such as fire and materials such as wood

8
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:18,700
are assigned relationships.

9
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:20,500
Although it is called a chemistry engine,

10
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:23,840
it does not simulate all real-world chemistry.

11
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:27,460
The first rule: an element changes a material’s state.

12
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:30,740
Fire touching wood changes it into a burning state.

13
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:33,480
A shared rule can apply in many locations.

14
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:34,120
The second rule:

15
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:37,780
elements can also change other elements’ states.

16
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:38,720
The relationship between water and fire

17
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,500
is handled within the same framework.

18
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:43,480
Beyond checking only burning objects,

19
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,760
effects themselves can interact.

20
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:48,880
The third rule concerns relationships between materials.

21
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:53,040
Within these chemistry rules, they do not directly change each other’s states.

22
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:55,420
That does not mean they cannot collide.

23
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:59,760
This distinguishes motion physics from the rules for state changes.

24
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:01,140
Treat rain as the water element,

25
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:04,520
and the existing water–fire relationship can be reused.

26
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:05,340
Electricity and wind also belong

27
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:08,300
within the game’s simplified state model.

28
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:10,680
As more rules can be combined,

29
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:13,720
players gain more combinations to try.

30
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:15,020
Use physics for movement

31
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:17,920
and shared rules for state changes.

32
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:20,120
Setting a fire and extinguishing it with rain

33
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:22,600
feel like consistent rules of one world.

34
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:25,540
The goal is more than producing many events:

35
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:28,040
create relationships that can be recombined.
